<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <PropertyGroup>
        <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
        <TargetFramework>net9.0</TargetFramework>
        <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
        <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
        <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>

        <!--
        AOT smoke test #5 (GH-4765) — the STORE-BACKED lane.

        Every other AOT smoke references Wolverine.csproj or Wolverine.Http
        alone, so no native image in CI has ever executed a chain that a
        PERSISTENCE package composed. That is the coverage gap GH-4765 stays
        open for, and the reason this whole class of bug has reached us from
        users' production publishes rather than from CI: GH-4752, GH-4778, and
        the EF Core frames fixed in #4803.

        Wolverine.Sqlite is the store that can hold the lane: a file, no server,
        so it costs a CI runner nothing to stand up. Fisher was the first
        candidate and is NOT usable yet — a Fisher document write cannot complete
        in a native image at all (fisher#384, two separate reflective-generics
        failures on the write path). Fisher also would not have tested the right
        thing: it composes saga chains through its own
        FisherPersistenceFrameProvider, so it never closes
        EnrollAndFetchSagaStorageFrame.

        What it pins: SqliteBackedPersistence registers
        LightweightSagaPersistenceFrameProvider as a PERSISTENCE STRATEGY, so
        SagaPersistenceChainPolicy closes
        EnrollAndFetchSagaStorageFrame<TId, TSaga> over the user's saga type
        during POLICY application — at startup, under TypeLoadMode.Static, with
        the generated code already compiled in. Nothing names that instantiation
        statically, so it survives only because the frame contributes its own
        root through IAotRootSource (GH-4765 / #4801). This project is the first
        committed-codegen project in the repo to have a saga at all, which is why
        #4801 and #4803 both came out byte-identical across the drift gate's
        seven projects and why neither was ever actually exercised end to end.

        Note that the same store ALSO registers SagaStorageVariableSource, which
        closes the identical frame from inside code generation. That path needs no
        root and cannot be given one — see the handoff note on
        HandlerGraph.aotRootsOf. Only the policy-placed close matters here.

        Carries NO hand-written roots, deliberately — same stance as
        Wolverine.AotSmoke.Http and the opposite of Wolverine.AotSmoke.Publish.
        A regression in the emitted rooting block has to fail this lane outright
        rather than hide behind an AotRoots.Pin() call.

        Refresh the committed pre-gen FROM THIS PROJECT'S DIRECTORY, because a
        console host writes relative to the working directory, not the project
        folder:

          dotnet run -f net9.0 codegen write

        then commit the result.
        -->
        <PublishAot>true</PublishAot>
        <StripSymbols>false</StripSymbols>
        <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>

        <!--
        GH-4805. Deliberately NOT setting JsonSerializerIsReflectionEnabledByDefault. PublishAot turns
        reflection-based System.Text.Json off, and this lane proves the saga path works with a
        source-generated JsonSerializerContext handed in through DatabaseSettings.SagaSerializerOptions
        instead - which is the seam that did not exist before GH-4805. Switching reflection back on would
        make this smoke pass without testing the thing it exists to test.
        -->

        <!-- Runtime behaviour of a native image is what this asserts. The trim-analysis warning wall is
             Wolverine.AotSmoke's job; here it would only bury the signal. Mirrors
             Wolverine.AotSmoke.Publish and Wolverine.AotSmoke.Http. -->
        <SuppressTrimAnalysisWarnings>true</SuppressTrimAnalysisWarnings>

        <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);IL2026;IL2046;IL2055;IL2065;IL2067;IL2070;IL2072;IL2075;IL2087;IL2090;IL2091;IL2104;IL2111;IL3000;IL3050;IL3051;IL3053</NoWarn>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Persistence\Wolverine.Sqlite\Wolverine.Sqlite.csproj" />
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
